From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: keep pmr735a L1 on for the modem

pmr735a_l1 is declared in this device tree and referenced by nothing, so
Linux never enables it. On the running device:

	 regulator      use open bypass  opmode voltage current     min     max
	    l1            0    0      0 unknown   576mV     0mA   576mV   648mV

Zero users, zero open, off, and parked at the bottom of the declared
range because no consumer votes a voltage. The stock tree holds the same
rail on, at 600 mV, with a 62 mA load declared against it. From the
extracted vendor device tree (blair, XT2225-1), verbatim:

	rpm-regulator-ldoe1 {
		compatible = "qcom,rpm-smd-regulator-resource";
		qcom,resource-name = "ldoe";
		qcom,resource-id = <0x01>;
		qcom,regulator-type = <0x00>;
		qcom,regulator-hw-type = "pmic5-ldo";
		qcom,hpm-min-load = <0x2710>;
		status = "okay";
		proxy-supply = <0x5e>;

		regulator-l1 {
			compatible = "qcom,rpm-smd-regulator";
			regulator-name = "pmr735a_l1";
			qcom,set = <0x03>;
			status = "okay";
			regulator-min-microvolt = <0x8b290>;
			regulator-max-microvolt = <0x9eb10>;
			qcom,init-voltage = <0x927c0>;
			qcom,proxy-consumer-enable;
			qcom,proxy-consumer-current = <0xf230>;
			phandle = <0x5e>;
		};
	};

In decimal that is 570000..650000 uV, an initial voltage of 600000, and a
proxy current of 62000 uA against an LDO whose high power mode threshold
(qcom,hpm-min-load) is 10000 uA.

The proxy-consumer properties are the vendor RPM regulator driver's way
of expressing "somebody needs this rail, and it is not anybody the
application processor can see". proxy-supply on the resource node points
at the regulator's own phandle, and at probe the driver takes a consumer
reference on itself: regulator_enable() plus regulator_set_load() with
qcom,proxy-consumer-current. There is no AP-side consumer to hand that
vote to, so the only thing that can want a 600 mV rail carrying tens of
milliamps is off the AP -- the modem's RF front end. A rail at that
voltage and that current is an RF/PLL supply, not a logic supply.

pmr735a_l1 is the only proxy-enabled rail in the whole stock tree with no
AP-side consumer node. Every other one -- pmr735a_s1_level (VDD_MX) and
pmr735a_s2_level (VDD_CX) -- is claimed by a clock controller or by
pil_modem, and mainline covers those through rpmpd.

Mainline has no proxy-consumer concept, so the constraint itself has to
say it:

  regulator-always-on	  the core enables the rail at registration and
			  never releases it, which is what the stock proxy
			  does for the whole of the vendor kernel's life.
			  Nothing on the AP will ever call regulator_enable()
			  on this rail, so there is nothing else that can.

  min == max == 600000	  pins qcom,init-voltage. With a range the core
			  settles on the floor, which is how the rail came
			  to read 576 mV; 600000 is an exact selector on the
			  pm660_nldo660 range (320000 + 35 * 8000).

  regulator-system-load	  the documented binding for "load present on this
			  regulator that is not captured by any consumer
			  request", which is precisely the 62 mA proxy.

  regulator-allow-set-load  without it drms_uA_update() returns early and
			  regulator-system-load is silently ignored, because
			  of_get_regulation_constraints() sets
			  REGULATOR_CHANGE_DRMS only for this property. The
			  load is what puts the LDO in high power mode
			  (hpm-min-load is 10 mA, the load is 62 mA), so
			  dropping it would enable the rail in whatever mode
			  RPM last left it in.

Together these produce, from rpm_reg_write_active(), the same single RPM
message the vendor's proxy produces on resource ldoe/1: swen=1, uv=600000,
ma=62.

regulator-boot-on is deliberately not added. It asserts that the
bootloader left the rail enabled, and that has not been measured here --
the rail reads disabled under mainline, but that is equally explained by
Linux having turned it off as unused. regulator-always-on alone gets the
rail to the same place without claiming something unverified.

Honest scope. This was written while chasing the reset in
docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/GNSS-SM6375.md, where bringing up the GNSS
measurement engine deasserts PS_HOLD within 100 ms and a cellid session
runs indefinitely. It is a candidate, not a demonstrated fix, and there
is a real argument against it: RPM aggregates requests per master, the
modem is an RPM master in its own right, and it can vote ldoe/1 for
itself without the AP's help. If the modem does vote for it, this patch
changes nothing about the reset. What is not in doubt is the narrower
claim -- the stock tree holds this rail and this port does not -- which
is a real difference between the two trees regardless of what it turns
out to explain, and it costs 4 lines and a rail that draws 62 mA when the
modem is up.

Signed-off-by: Kali NetHunter rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -337,9 +337,17 @@
 		 * S2 is VDD_CX
 		 */
 
+		/*
+		 * No AP consumer: the modem's RF front end is the load. The
+		 * vendor holds it with qcom,proxy-consumer-enable and
+		 * qcom,proxy-consumer-current = <62000>.
+		 */
 		pmr735a_l1: l1 {
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <570000>;
-			regulator-max-microvolt = <650000>;
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <600000>;
+			regulator-max-microvolt = <600000>;
+			regulator-always-on;
+			regulator-allow-set-load;
+			regulator-system-load = <62000>;
 		};
 
 		pmr735a_l2: l2 {
